Starvation is a condition in which a process waits indefinitely for CPU time 
because other higher-priority 
processes continuously receive the CPU. In the given multilevel queue scheduler, 
here are three queues:
Q1 (Highest Priority): Interactive Processes (Time Quantum = 5 ms)
Q2: System Processes (Time Quantum = 10 ms)
Q3: Batch Processes (FCFS)
Process P5 is placed in Q3, the lowest-priority queue. The scheduler always gives
preference to processes in Q1 and Q2. 
If new interactive or system processes keep arriving, they are executed first, 
preventing P5 from receiving CPU time.
As a result, P5 remains in the ready queue for an indefinite period, leading to starvation.
The root cause of starvation is the strict priority scheduling used by the
multilevel queue scheduler. 
Since higher-priority queues are always served before lower-priority queues, 
the batch process in Q3 is continuously postponed. 
Although P5 is ready to execute, it never gets a chance because higher-priority 
processes keep entering the system.
Optimizations
1. Aging Technique
Implement aging, where the priority of a process is gradually increased 
as its waiting time increases. 
If P5 waits for a long time in Q3, its priority is increased, allowing 
it to move to a higher-priority queue and eventually receive CPU time. 
Aging is the most effective method to prevent starvation.
2. Fair CPU Scheduling
Allocate a fixed percentage of CPU time to each queue or periodically 
schedule processes from lower-priority queues. 
This ensures that even batch processes in Q3 receive CPU time, improving 
fairness and preventing indefinite postponement.
Conclusion
The starvation occurs because P5 belongs to the lowest-priority queue (Q3), 
while higher-priority queues continuously receive CPU time. 
Implementing aging and fair CPU scheduling ensures that every process eventually 
gets CPU time, preventing starvation and improving overall system fairness.


5.A context switch occurs when the operating system saves the state of the 
currently running process and loads the state of another process.
Although context switching is necessary for multitasking, excessive context 
switching reduces system performance 
because the CPU spends more time switching between processes than executing them.
In the given server:
20,000 context switches per second
Average CPU burst time = 2 ms
Very high I/O wait frequency
Preemptive schedulerTime quantum = 1 ms
The main reason for the high number of context switches is the very small time quantum. 
Since the average CPU burst time is 2 ms, 
but the scheduler allows a process to run for only 1 ms, almost every process is 
interrupted before completing its CPU burst. 
This causes frequent preemption and increases the number of context switches.
Another reason is the high I/O wait frequency. Processes frequently move from the
Running state to the Waiting state to perform I/O operations. 
Once the I/O operation is complete, they return to the Ready state and must be scheduled again.
These repeated transitions create additional context switches and increase CPU overhead.
Optimizations
1. Increase the Time Quantum
Increase the time quantum from 1 ms to a larger value such as 4 ms or 5 ms. 
A larger quantum allows processes to complete their CPU bursts with fewer interruptions, 
reducing unnecessary context switches and improving CPU efficiency.
2. Reduce I/O Waiting
Use techniques such as asynchronous I/O, buffering, caching, or
thread pools to reduce the number of I/O operations and waiting time. 
This decreases the frequency of process state changes and lowers the number of context switches.
Conclusion
The high context-switch overhead is caused by the very small time quantum and frequent I/O waits. 
Increasing the time quantum and optimizing I/O operations significantly reduce context switching,
improve CPU utilization, and enhance the overall performance of the server.
